        {
          "title": "Check yourself",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "qa",
              "q": "`s` has daily values 1, 2, 4 on Jan 1, Jan 2 and Jan 4. What does `s.shift(1)` put on Jan 4?",
              "a_bullets": [
                "**2**, the value from Jan 2",
                "shift moves ==rows==, not days",
                "Call `asfreq('D')` first to expose the Jan 3 gap"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "qa",
              "q": "`pd.to_datetime('03/04/2024')` is meant to be 3 April. Which date do you get, and what is the fix?",
              "a_bullets": [
                "{red:March 4}, because month comes first by default",
                "Pass `dayfirst=True` or an explicit `format='%d/%m/%Y'`"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "qa",
              "q": "`pd.Timestamp('2024-03-01 09:00').tz_convert('UTC')` fails. Why, and how do you fix it?",
              "a_bullets": [
                "The timestamp is naive, so `tz_convert` raises ==TypeError==",
                "Run `tz_localize('Asia/Kolkata')` first, then `tz_convert('UTC')`",
                "Result: 03:30 UTC"
              ],
              "example_code": [
                "ts.tz_localize('Asia/Kolkata').tz_convert('UTC')"
              ]
            }
